WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
Sujata Day, Actor, Writer/Director: The&nbsp;
HR lady who I had become friends with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:08.160
she was really sad and disappointed that&nbsp;
she had to lay me off and I was like…

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:14.080
Film Courage: To anyone working&nbsp;
in the film industry Sujata&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.640
if they had to put a time limit on becoming&nbsp;
a working professional what would that be?

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:24.560
Sujata: Never put a time limit on art I would say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.760
because I have found throughout my years there&nbsp;
have been a lot of my friends and acquaintances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:39.280
that I've been in touch with or met in acting&nbsp;
class or through writing and anyone who puts a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:49.520
time limit on it such as if I don't do this thing&nbsp;
within a year or if I don't do this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:56.480
goal within two or three years then I'm going&nbsp;
to go back to XYZ which could be anything which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:03.680
could be in my case you know I have an engineering&nbsp;
degree so if I were to say that okay I need to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:12.240
to direct a studio film within five years or else&nbsp;
I'm going to go back to engineering I feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.480
the universe hears that and listens to that&nbsp;
and says okay you have this backup plan so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.280
it won't give you that first thing that you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.720
and I just think it's a… everybody says&nbsp;
this but it's a marathon not a sprint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:35.360
and I've noticed that there are goals that I hope&nbsp;
to have wished to achieve by this point which I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:42.080
haven't achieved but I'm enjoying the journey and&nbsp;
the journey has been so much fun. It's all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:48.160
waking up in the morning being excited&nbsp;
that you actually get to pursue your dream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:56.400
and enjoying the moment that you're in whether&nbsp;
it's oh you get to go pitch to a certain executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:03.280
or studio or network today or whether it's you get&nbsp;
to go have coffee with a certain writer that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:11.280
are inspired by and and just taking those little&nbsp;
experiences and making that exciting and making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:19.280
that a goal as opposed to I need to sell a show&nbsp;
by this date or I need to be a series regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:29.200
by this date so I truly believe you can't put a&nbsp;
time limit on your dreams when you were getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:36.560
that engineering degree was that your plan a&nbsp;
at the time no that was always my plan B so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:49.520
I’ll take it back to my childhood where I was&nbsp;
always dancing, singing, writing, acting but I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:57.440
also really good at math and science it came easy&nbsp;
to me and when I entered middle school I started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.400
doing plays when I entered high school I started&nbsp;
doing musicals and I was always dancing every week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:09.040
and my parents were very supportive and they&nbsp;
would come to Guys and Dolls every night and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.440
bring a bouquet of flowers and cheer for me&nbsp;
and it was always really exciting but then I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:23.360
was also taking AP physics, AP bio, AP chem, AP&nbsp;
calculus and killing it on those exams as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:33.600
so my plan my master plan I started thinking about&nbsp;
when I was about 16 or 17 and I said to myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:40.960
okay I can't move to Hollywood at the age of 17.&nbsp;
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to go get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:50.880
this engineering degree and take it from there and&nbsp;
then move out to Los Angeles after I graduate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.880
so I went to get my engineering degree and in the&nbsp;
meantime continued to do plays and musicals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:03.600
continue to write in college I took a semester of&nbsp;
screenwriting I took a semester of playwriting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:12.080
then I the summer before my senior year of college&nbsp;
I interned at a company called Accenture which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:19.600
a global consulting firm really big name they're&nbsp;
on par with Deloitte Touche and Ernst and Young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:27.840
and had a fun summer interning there and at the&nbsp;
end of that summer I was offered a full-time job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.920
so this was even before going into my senior year&nbsp;
so they asked me where I wanted to work what city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:40.720
I wanted to work in and I said Los Angeles and&nbsp;
I said Los Angeles and they said great that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.440
where we'll send you after you graduate so I went&nbsp;
into my senior year already having a job and as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:56.160
soon as I graduated I moved to LA on Accenture's&nbsp;
dime and then I was on a salary so I was making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:04.720
a load of money at the age of 20 pretty much and&nbsp;
the thing about consulting is you never have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.920
work unless you're placed on a project so&nbsp;
basically that entire year that I worked there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:17.600
I was only on a project for about two weeks&nbsp;
and I was the worst Accenture employee you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.640
could ever think of and I just had all my phone&nbsp;
calls go to my cell phone, never went into work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.960
and in the meantime what I was doing was meeting&nbsp;
with agents, I was going to acting class, I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.400
getting headshots, I was auditioning and at&nbsp;
the end of that year working for Accenture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:45.040
I got laid off which was also awesome&nbsp;
because I got severance, I got unemployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:52.480
and for about eight to ten months I believe&nbsp;
I was making money and that's when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:00.320
ramped up my auditioning and about I don't know&nbsp;
months later I booked three national commercials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:06.640
in a row so that's when my professional career&nbsp;
started I believe and in the meantime I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.720
still writing scripts, still writing shorts,&nbsp;
writing short stories, poems, songs, etc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:20.080
and that's how I got into acting but it was all&nbsp;
part of the master plan of like okay I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:26.080
want to be a burden to my parents I want to&nbsp;
be able to support myself what's the best way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:32.480
I can do that okay let's take this job in this&nbsp;
other field which is kind of the perfect day job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:39.760
to be honest and then I moved out here and&nbsp;
did it on my own how did you get word that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:46.960
were being laid off what was hilarious was I&nbsp;
was like I told you I was a terrible employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.600
so it was a round of layoffs a bunch of&nbsp;
employees were getting laid off at the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.520
and I was one of them I came in and the human&nbsp;
resources lady who I had become friends with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:04.720
she was really sad and disappointed that she&nbsp;
had to lay me off and I was like I'm good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:13.040
please don't waste your emotions on&nbsp;
me and and it was it was a good day.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:18.320
Film Courage: How many plan B’s have you&nbsp;
had while you've been here in Los Angeles?

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.920
Sujata: I’ve had zero plan B’s. I've had&nbsp;
the day jobs which have been really fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:30.400
actually I think there was always a dream of me&nbsp;
even being an engineer the dream that you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:39.280
in tv and in the movies of an aspiring actor is&nbsp;
they're working at a restaurant or they're working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:47.840
as a hostess somewhere and that's what I did and&nbsp;
I had so much fun because I was finally surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:54.240
by creative people because everyone working in the&nbsp;
restaurant business in Los Angeles generally tends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:01.520
to be an artist and that is their day job so I had&nbsp;
a blast I worked at a diner I worked at a fancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:07.680
steakhouse and then I realized that I really loved&nbsp;
hostessing so I was working the front of desk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.280
at these places and it was really fun I was&nbsp;
meeting all these people working at really hot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:23.760
trendy spots in la and making friends and I never&nbsp;
considered that a plan B that was always just me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:32.000
making friends and making my home in Los Angeles&nbsp;
surrounded by the people that I finally wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.120
be surrounded by. Film Courage: Yeah those can be&nbsp;
fun jobs, I know they can be stressful though too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.800
Especially if you think that there is no time&nbsp;
limit for them. I think they're romantic for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:47.920
a while but there's also a trap of being&nbsp;
in them too long. I think that can really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:54.000
weigh people down. How do you handle or how did&nbsp;
you at that time well-meaning comments maybe from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:00.480
family and friends that we're like that's great&nbsp;
you know we love that you're still pursuing like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:06.560
you're dancing and acting but you're gonna need&nbsp;
something more stable or you can't make real money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:14.320
from this things like that? Sujata: Well coming&nbsp;
from an Indian immigrant family you get those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:21.760
comments a little bit more maybe than usual and&nbsp;
I just handled it by I’d be like well this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:26.720
money that I made on the commercial and so I’ll&nbsp;
just book another commercial and we'll see what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:32.240
what happens obviously it's not that easy you&nbsp;
don't just go out and book another commercial but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:41.760
I was making steady progress so it did something&nbsp;
that my my dad would say was why don't you go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:49.200
get a Master’s in acting or it's always about&nbsp;
more education and and I was like well you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:56.720
I'm taking Upright Citizens Brigade classes and&nbsp;
I'm taking really fun other drama type acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:03.040
classes and I'm taking sketch writing classes and&nbsp;
everything I write like I get a little bit better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:10.480
every script I write is a little bit better than&nbsp;
the one before and so I just really had to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:16.560
to my parents like this is what I'm doing, I'm&nbsp;
continuously studying, I'm continuously growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:22.560
and I don't necessarily need to go do a&nbsp;
Master's program somewhere. Film Courage:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:27.680
So you showed them like here's a residual&nbsp;
check or here's whatever the buyout check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:33.040
that I got for this commercial and maybe that&nbsp;
quelled some of their anxiety? Sujata: Yeah I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:39.280
think it was more about can you pay the rent? Can&nbsp;
you pay for your food? Are you eating healthy?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:47.920
Are you out on the street somewhere and so it was&nbsp;
mostly about just having a stable life as opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:55.840
to I think their version of the Hollywood&nbsp;
dream was more people living on the streets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.840
and doing shady things to succeed and I was like&nbsp;
you know that's in your soap operas that you watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.120
but that's not real life. Hollywood there's just&nbsp;
a lot of people who are working really hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:13.840
achieve their dreams and continuously growing in&nbsp;
terms of their art and that's what I want to do.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:16.796
What do you think about putting&nbsp;
a time limit on your dreams?

